    "answer": {
      "short": "The original number cancels: $2(x+3)-2x=6$, so multiplying by 7 always gives $42$.",
      "worked": "Let the original number be $x$. The final result is $7(2(x+3)-2x)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}7(2(x+3)-2x)&=7(2x+6-2x)\\\\7(2(x+3)-2x)&=7\\times6\\\\7(2(x+3)-2x)&=42\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe $2x$ and $-2x$ cancel, so the result is 42 regardless of the original number.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
